1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Torrential rains reveal engineering flaws in Southern California.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Million dollar homes slide down a cliff, and a major road becomes a gigantic sinkhole.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
In France, the roof of a monumental airport terminal collapses.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Off the coast of Virginia, an aging coal ship sinks in rough seas, killing 31 mariners.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
A deadly plane crash changes the face of passenger aviation.

6
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:35,180
And a tiny imperfection creates big problems for one of the most acclaimed structures of the 21st century.

7
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,920
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,800
Persistent heavy rains wreaked havoc throughout Southern California.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,760
during the winter of 2004-2005.

10
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:06,560
Los Angeles received 37 and a quarter inches of rain that season,

11
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,540
making it the wettest year the City of Angels had seen in 121 years.

12
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,680
But months would pass before the rains caused some of the worst damage.

13
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:21,980
On the perfectly sunny morning of June 1st, 2005,

14
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:25,560
residents of Bluebird Canyon in Laguna Beach, California,

15
00:01:25,561 --> 00:01:27,540
were awakened by strange noises.

16
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,980
I heard really loud popping.

17
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,540
It almost sounded like a BB gun, like kids in the canyon.

18
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:37,500
And when I realized it was coming from under the house is when I kind of panicked.

19
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,280
So at this point, I ran back to the front of the house.

20
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:46,200
I opened the front door, and I actually saw my curb separating from my walkway.

21
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,900
We could feel the house moving, and it was very loud, the cracking, the popping.

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,380
And we ran outside.

23
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,760
I think we were the first neighbors out, but they quickly followed.

24
00:01:56,761 --> 00:02:01,160
Everyone was screaming, asking what happened, what happened, what's going on.

25
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:07,460
What was going on was that a seven-acre wedge of land was breaking away from the hillside.

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
It would send these residents and their homes sliding down the slope.

27
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,660
Houses cracked.

28
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:15,460
Streets buckled.

29
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:19,060
Residents ran for their lives.

30
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:24,300
And I grabbed my two-year-old out of the crib.

31
00:02:24,301 --> 00:02:26,580
I just pulled my arm.

32
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:27,780
I knew it right then.

33
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:29,000
The house was sliding.

34
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:30,380
I didn't know what to do,

35
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,340
so I just ran into my four-year-old's room

36
00:02:32,341 --> 00:02:33,480
and I put out my arm and I said,

37
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:34,560
get up and grab mommy's arm.

38
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:35,700
I said, the house is breaking.

39
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:38,640
Well, I guess, you know,

40
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,560
you might say all hell broke loose.

41
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:42,240
We tried to get off the street,

42
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,800
but as we tried to go down the road,

43
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:46,520
the street actually opened up.

44
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,780
It rose probably about 30 feet into the air.

45
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:52,660
What we didn't realize was that the street was falling.

46
00:02:52,661 --> 00:02:55,740
that these houses were coming down.

47
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:00,020
Flamingo Road, located near the top of the hill,

48
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:03,520
and the houses on it plunged about 75 feet.

49
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:09,960
What happened is over a half a million cubic yards of earth

50
00:03:09,961 --> 00:03:13,860
gave way and literally avalanched down the hill

51
00:03:13,861 --> 00:03:15,460
into the bottom of Bluebird Canyon.

52
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,280
And that's about the same amount of dirt

53
00:03:19,281 --> 00:03:22,020
that would fill over 10,000 swimming pools.

54
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:26,520
It's a lot of mass moving very fast with a lot of energy.

55
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:31,340
Now, when this landslide first occurred, many of my colleagues were kind of surprised.

56
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:34,460
It wasn't a rainy day. I'm used to landslides happening on a rainy day.

57
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,700
Well, deep-seated landslides like this don't necessarily happen on a rainy day.

58
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:42,720
They happen when the water table has been elevated,

59
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:47,460
and it takes many months for water to percolate down deep into the Earth.

60
00:03:47,461 --> 00:03:53,600
Usually, seasonal bursts of heavy rain dump 12 to 13 inches on Southern California.

61
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:55,880
The rain runs off before it's absorbed.

62
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:03,520
However, the 30-plus inches of rain that soaked the area during 2004-2005

63
00:04:03,521 --> 00:04:07,600
was a persistent rain over a long period of time,

64
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,780
saturating the earth from October to May.

65
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:16,180
This landslide is a bedrock landslide,

66
00:04:16,181 --> 00:04:20,180
And the bedrock in this area is what we call the Topanga Formation.

67
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:23,180
And it's a mixture of sands and silts and clays.

68
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:29,180
In particular, there's a layer at a depth of anywhere from 50 to 80 feet.

69
00:04:29,181 --> 00:04:33,180
The clay that the landslide failed on is a very plastic clay.

70
00:04:33,181 --> 00:04:37,180
And by that I mean when you get it wet, it's a consistency of play-dough.

71
00:04:37,181 --> 00:04:39,180
It's not very high-strength material.

72
00:04:39,181 --> 00:04:43,180
So when you tilt that up and then you compound the problem

73
00:04:43,181 --> 00:04:48,780
the problem by having a very heavy rainfall year, twice normal rain, that builds up water

74
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:52,680
into this and softens the clay. That led to the failure.

75
00:04:52,681 --> 00:05:00,600
The deep landslide claimed 20 homes, but miraculously, no lives.

76
00:05:00,601 --> 00:05:08,140
The interior structures of the homes are just crumbled into hundreds and thousands of pieces.

77
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:14,340
fallen down, granite countertops broken in half, cabinets ripped off of walls.

78
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:18,440
It's complete devastation in most of these homes that you see behind me.

79
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:25,120
I would call this an engineering disaster simply from the failure to recognize that this was a hazardous area.

80
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:31,560
That's a great challenge, and it's one thing that geologists need to do whenever they investigate an area for new development.

81
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:37,500
But clearly in this case, in retrospect, we can tell that this was an unsuitable place to build houses.

82
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:41,820
This was not the first time a hillside gave way in Bluebird Canyon.

83
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:46,880
A huge landslide consumed part of the neighborhood on October 1st, 1978,

84
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,880
just a few hundred feet from the 2005 landslide.

85
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:53,840
24 families lost homes then.

86
00:05:55,280 --> 00:06:01,400
Most of the homes on the hillside were constructed before building codes were strict, as they are today.

87
00:06:01,401 --> 00:06:12,040
At the time these houses were built, there really wasn't any kind of geotechnical analysis or geologic assessment of stability.

88
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:17,000
They more or less just came along and graded a flat pad and built their houses.

89
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,780
I'm confident that they didn't know what kind of risks that they were facing.

90
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:30,960
In August 2005, red-tagged homes were demolished and a massive hillside reconstruction project began.

91
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:38,400
We're attacking this slide stabilization in several phases.

92
00:06:38,401 --> 00:06:44,400
First, the most important things we have to do are to get the canyon opened up and put in a storm drain system.

93
00:06:44,401 --> 00:06:53,400
And secondly, we're going to be stabilizing the headscarp area so that we don't lose more homes and possibly a street up above those homes.

94
00:06:53,401 --> 00:06:56,400
We'll be putting in caissons and tiebacks up there.

95
00:06:56,401 --> 00:07:01,920
and we'll also be removing all the homes on the landslide and regrading it to seal the fissures

96
00:07:01,921 --> 00:07:06,140
and try to establish drainage so that we don't end up with a lot of mud and debris flows.

97
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:13,860
When we're all done with the repair plan, we will have put back the slope that was there before,

98
00:07:13,861 --> 00:07:14,939
but way better.

99
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:20,260
It will be anchored into the bedrock like it should have been in the first

100
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:28,100
place and we'll have put in with it proper storm drains that will, when it rains hard,

101
00:07:28,101 --> 00:07:34,320
will collect that runoff and get it underground and away so that it doesn't saturate the soil

102
00:07:34,321 --> 00:07:41,000
and mobilize it again and it will be a perfectly safe building site following current building

103
00:07:41,001 --> 00:07:43,220
codes today.

104
00:07:43,221 --> 00:07:48,760
The cost of the repair plan is estimated at over $10 million to be paid by the state,

105
00:07:48,761 --> 00:07:51,760
the city of Laguna Beach and Bluebird Canyon residents.

106
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:58,620
Most residents plan to move back onto the hillside once it's repaired,

107
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:01,180
placing their faith in the skill of engineers.

108
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:06,100
When you wonder whether homes should be built in places like this at all,

109
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:10,740
when they have discovered these kind of geological risks,

110
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,500
I think the answer still is yes.

111
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:16,660
Carefully, surgically,

112
00:08:16,661 --> 00:08:22,840
as far as this disaster goes, it is a disaster because of a lack of engineering.

113
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:27,880
Had there been proper engineering before and during the house construction,

114
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:29,620
this would have been prevented.

115
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:34,220
While this disaster came months after the Los Angeles storms,

116
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:39,160
other engineering disasters occurred during the height of the rains.

117
00:08:39,161 --> 00:08:53,161
On February 19, 2005, after weeks of downpours, rainwater filled a crack on busy Tujunga Avenue in Sun Valley, California, a part of Los Angeles.

118
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,120
Quickly, the crack expanded.

119
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:02,980
When I first heard about the hole, I was just starting to leave work on a Saturday morning,

120
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:08,760
and we noticed that the sidewalk about where it starts right there was starting to cave in.

121
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:14,940
Within a few hours, the fissure had turned into a 30-foot-long fault that bisected the road.

122
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:19,440
Initially, the hole measured about 40 feet in diameter.

123
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:22,420
But the sinkhole kept growing.

124
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:29,080
The rushing water and falling chunks of roadway crushed a sewer pipe and severed a power line,

125
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:31,680
turning the roadway into a virtual river.

126
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:37,200
We came back on Sunday to help them to fill it with concrete.

127
00:09:37,201 --> 00:09:43,960
It had already caved in under the street and it was starting to go back gradually as it rained more and more.

128
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:46,420
It was raining the hardest I've ever seen since I worked here.

129
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:54,020
A neighboring business provided city workers with 250 cubic yards of concrete to help fill the sinkhole.

130
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:58,680
We were trying to pour concrete inside of the piece that was missing.

131
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:05,460
But every time we poured it in, the water was just washed away because the water was coming so fast and so hard that it didn't have a chance to stick.

132
00:10:05,461 --> 00:10:11,220
The chasm eventually swallowed more than 200 feet of pavement.

133
00:10:11,221 --> 00:10:16,420
250,000 cubic yards of soil would eventually wash away.

134
00:10:16,421 --> 00:10:23,180
To make things worse, Tujunga Avenue was flanked by a landfill on one side and a sand and gravel

135
00:10:23,181 --> 00:10:26,520
pit the size of a football field on the other.

136
00:10:26,521 --> 00:10:29,780
The growing sinkhole joined the existing pit.

137
00:10:29,781 --> 00:10:33,780
At 10.30 p.m. on February 20th,

138
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:36,180
Public Works Supervisor Rory Shaw

139
00:10:36,181 --> 00:10:39,240
was monitoring his workers as they repaired the sinkhole.

140
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:42,800
Suddenly, the ground he was standing on caved in.

141
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:45,000
I'm watching him from the other side,

142
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:47,560
and no more, all I hear is this noise

143
00:10:47,561 --> 00:10:50,900
and the rumbling of the metal and the wash and the rapids.

144
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:53,780
The truck was parked on a piece of the road that was hanging over,

145
00:10:54,340 --> 00:10:57,400
and when it started to crack and they could hear the road breaking away,

146
00:10:57,560 --> 00:10:58,840
he tried to run up to the truck.

147
00:10:58,841 --> 00:11:04,240
I believe when he got in or he got up next to the truck, the street just caved in and the truck fell in the hole.

148
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:09,760
Rory Shaw died in a raging torrent, falling victim to a failure of engineering.

149
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:20,860
Tujunga Avenue had been built over a dry riverbed, without an underground pipe system or curbside channels to divert rainwaters.

150
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:28,560
In Southern California, dry river or creek beds called arroyos are subject to winter flooding.

151
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:33,960
A key point to understand is there is no such thing as a former riverbed.

152
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:37,040
Water continues to flow into the area.

153
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:39,860
Water simply doesn't flow on the surface.

154
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:41,420
It has to go somewhere.

155
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:48,160
Or it's going to percolate through the sand and through the gravel, whatever else is under there.

156
00:11:48,260 --> 00:11:49,180
It will percolate.

157
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:56,500
Or if you have in your road a significant crack, as we often do have in asphalt pavement,

158
00:11:56,501 --> 00:12:03,940
And that water, with its energy, will be scouring the underlying bedding through that crack.

159
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:15,560
Once you start at a crack, that crack now from the edge of the road will be unzipping that crack upstream to where the water is coming from.

160
00:12:15,561 --> 00:12:19,720
And eventually, you will have this huge, huge, massive hole.

161
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:23,800
Still, the placement of Tujunga Avenue is not unique.

162
00:12:23,801 --> 00:12:30,340
The bottom of the canyon, or at least close to the bottom of the canyon, is always an

163
00:12:30,341 --> 00:12:34,020
attractive place to run a street, run a road.

164
00:12:34,021 --> 00:12:37,680
Historically, people did it for thousands of years.

165
00:12:37,681 --> 00:12:44,240
But what needs to be taken into consideration is the fact that those streets do become a

166
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:52,740
water collector, and that they cannot be left to their own devices in handling of that water.

167
00:12:53,800 --> 00:13:01,840
Today, the hole remains, a visible reminder of the damage caused by heavy rains and lack of drainage on Tohunga Avenue.

168
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:09,300
The city estimates it will cost up to $100 million to repair the hole and build a proper flood control infrastructure.

169
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:11,620
That could take years.

170
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:24,900
From 1969 to 2004, presidential disaster declarations were issued for 60 landslides, 12 of which occurred in California.

171
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:31,580
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

172
00:13:31,581 --> 00:13:45,100
On June 25, 2003, France celebrated the grand opening of Terminal 2E at Charles de Gaulle

173
00:13:45,101 --> 00:13:46,100
Airport in Paris.

174
00:13:47,320 --> 00:13:53,380
However, an engineering catastrophe would turn jubilation into mourning before its first

175
00:13:53,381 --> 00:13:54,081
anniversary.

176
00:13:55,960 --> 00:14:00,540
Terminal 2E was part of a projected state-of-the-art four-terminal hub for Air France.

177
00:14:00,541 --> 00:14:07,160
It was being built to handle up to 17 planes at once and 10 million passengers a year.

178
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:12,900
Aeroport de Paris, or ADP, the airport authority for the greater Paris region,

179
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:18,480
in collaboration with Air France, spent almost $1 billion to build the innovative structure,

180
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:21,960
made to show off French engineering skill and panache.

181
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:29,540
It's just a joyous space, and you need a joyous space

182
00:14:29,541 --> 00:14:33,523
When you're coming off the transatlantic flight at 7 a.m.

183
00:14:33,524 --> 00:14:41,280
and you're going to be in the airport for a few hours waiting for another flight out, the architecture matters.

184
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:52,680
A structure with no interior columns, the futuristic tunnel was admired by many, including travelers and workers who arrived at the terminal on May 23, 2004.

185
00:14:52,681 --> 00:14:57,600
11 months after opening celebrations

186
00:14:57,601 --> 00:15:09,060
suddenly several tons of concrete steel and glass collapsed as a 98 foot section of air

187
00:15:09,061 --> 00:15:17,320
france's boarding lounge roof fell in it's very long but it's also huge in the span is nearly 100

188
00:15:17,321 --> 00:15:23,834
feet wide. The concrete on one side slid off the support and fell to the ground.

189
00:15:23,835 --> 00:15:25,300
On the other side,

190
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:34,380
the concrete literally flattens the roof of the oval comes to the floor now, sits on the floor

191
00:15:34,381 --> 00:15:41,839
there. Huge amount of concrete. Remember, we are talking one foot thick concrete.

192
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,220
So that's a lot

193
00:15:43,221 --> 00:15:46,940
of weight. That's 60 pounds per square foot.

194
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,480
When the heavy concrete roof crashed, it killed four people and injured three others.

195
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:58,879
The collapse astonished many engineers and designers.

196
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:01,160
Just months before, the terminal

197
00:16:01,161 --> 00:16:05,400
had been praised for its technical prowess. How could it fail?

198
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:09,460
The clues lie in the startling design.

199
00:16:09,461 --> 00:16:17,960
The roof of the boarding lounge consisted of a concrete vault, pierced with openings to allow light to pass through,

200
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:21,960
and an exterior metallic structure on which the glasswork was fixed.

201
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:24,520
Pillars supported the entire construction.

202
00:16:27,160 --> 00:16:32,780
The oval-shaped structure sat inside a glass shell, where the temperature and environment were constant.

203
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:38,980
The glass encasing rested on tensioning cables that kept the oval from bulging out.

204
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:44,700
It's a structure that gets stronger the more you press on it.

205
00:16:44,701 --> 00:16:51,220
And the classic compression structure that people know about is an egg.

206
00:16:51,780 --> 00:16:58,680
If you think about an egg, very, very thin shell compared to the size and weight of the egg.

207
00:16:59,100 --> 00:17:02,840
And yet it's actually pretty strong. It takes some work to crack that egg.

208
00:17:02,841 --> 00:17:11,940
On the other hand, if you get an egg that has a crack in it, that did develop a crack, it's very weak structure.

209
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:18,700
On this unusually cold May morning, the terminal's concrete structure was extremely fragile.

210
00:17:20,020 --> 00:17:26,560
Prior to the collapse, the temperature dropped sharply from 77 degrees Fahrenheit to just 39 degrees.

211
00:17:26,561 --> 00:17:35,120
The cables are exposed to fluctuating outdoor temperatures that cause them to expand and

212
00:17:35,121 --> 00:17:38,220
contract more than the concrete.

213
00:17:38,221 --> 00:17:43,920
On a hot day, the cables expand and slacken, reducing structural support and increasing

214
00:17:43,921 --> 00:17:46,520
the likelihood that cracks will form.

215
00:17:46,521 --> 00:17:52,140
Now comes an evening after a hot day.

216
00:17:52,141 --> 00:17:54,880
The cable suddenly cools down.

217
00:17:54,881 --> 00:17:59,360
We know that just prior to the collapse there was a massive drop in temperatures.

218
00:17:59,361 --> 00:18:01,800
The cable shortens.

219
00:18:01,801 --> 00:18:03,600
What happens when the cable shortens?

220
00:18:03,601 --> 00:18:05,980
It wants to straighten out.

221
00:18:05,981 --> 00:18:10,780
It cannot straighten out because the struts are holding it in the position where it is.

222
00:18:10,781 --> 00:18:14,700
The force in the struts is increasing dramatically.

223
00:18:14,701 --> 00:18:20,900
But the concrete was designed to handle the extra force from the struts.

224
00:18:20,901 --> 00:18:26,100
But when you start dealing with those changes in the forces, in the strut that I described

225
00:18:26,101 --> 00:18:32,900
to you, related to temperature, related to the concrete now, when it cracks, it becomes

226
00:18:32,901 --> 00:18:36,480
much more flexible, so it's no longer the rigid structure.

227
00:18:36,481 --> 00:18:44,480
The strut punches through, the concrete shell loses its support, and goes flat.

228
00:18:44,481 --> 00:18:45,840
And that's what happened.

229
00:18:45,841 --> 00:18:51,500
The perfect eggshell was further compromised by necessary structural elements of the terminal.

230
00:18:52,700 --> 00:19:00,960
In the section that collapsed, you have three very large entryways right next to one another,

231
00:19:01,100 --> 00:19:06,140
three protrusions that mar the perfect smoothness of the shell.

232
00:19:07,040 --> 00:19:10,380
That disturbs the symmetry of the structure.

233
00:19:10,381 --> 00:19:16,080
No longer can the structure redistribute the loads freely.

234
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:22,780
If one area gets slightly overloaded, you have perfect symmetry, it will shift the load elsewhere.

235
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:29,460
But if you have those sharp discontinuities, such as a hole cut in it,

236
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:38,440
that's no longer a nice and self-adjusting oval symmetric structure.

237
00:19:39,140 --> 00:19:43,440
In February 2005, 20 months after the collapse,

238
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:48,040
the French Minister of Transport released the findings of an administrative investigation.

239
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:55,040
The report stated that the structure had been slowly deteriorating since its inauguration in June 2003.

240
00:19:56,120 --> 00:20:00,140
And they were very, very careful about spreading blame.

241
00:20:00,141 --> 00:20:04,140
Well, the metal wasn't quite right with the concrete.

242
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:07,580
The foundation wasn't quite perfect.

243
00:20:07,581 --> 00:20:18,920
The design had a few little problems in it, but almost certainly the failure started where the shell was pierced massively from the side.

244
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:26,180
In May 2005, ADP announced that it would rebuild the damaged roof of Terminal 2E.

245
00:20:28,620 --> 00:20:36,360
Pierre Graf, president of ADP, said that the only way to ensure passenger safety was to replace the entire curved roof shell.

246
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:46,880
The completely renovated Terminal 2E is slated to reopen later in the decade and will cost an estimated $195 million.

247
00:20:51,060 --> 00:20:56,320
Terminal 2E was designed by internationally renowned French architect Paul Androux.

248
00:20:56,860 --> 00:21:05,880
Coincidentally, he also designed Terminal 3 at Dubai International Airport, which collapsed during a construction accident on September 28, 2004.

249
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:14,580
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

250
00:21:14,581 --> 00:21:23,580
On February 12, 1983, a routine shipping run from Norfolk, Virginia to Braden Point, Massachusetts,

251
00:21:23,581 --> 00:21:30,580
turned into a fatal voyage and one of America's worst maritime disasters.

252
00:21:30,581 --> 00:21:32,288
It was a bad storm.

253
00:21:32,289 --> 00:21:38,400
The weather bureau called it the worst East Coast storm in 40 years.

254
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:45,940
The Marine Electric bulk carrier, similar to this ship, was transporting more than 24,000 tons of coal.

255
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:49,320
A crew of 34 merchant mariners were aboard.

256
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:55,920
Most of the crew and officers considered the Marine Electric a real milk run.

257
00:21:55,921 --> 00:22:00,180
They always knew that they were only 30 miles off the coast.

258
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:03,740
And if something went wrong, the Coast Guard could come out and get them.

259
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:09,920
But as the Marine Electric left the Chesapeake Bay in the early morning hours of February 11th,

260
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:13,800
bad weather off the coast was setting a fatal scenario in motion.

261
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:18,740
Then, in the dead of night on February 12th,

262
00:22:19,940 --> 00:22:23,800
the situation aboard the coal carrier went from uneasy to desperate.

263
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:29,620
And it was about 1.30 when the ship was getting into trouble.

264
00:22:29,621 --> 00:22:30,321
the seas.

265
00:22:30,322 --> 00:22:35,060
They were breaking, coming right down the deck, down to number two and three

266
00:22:35,061 --> 00:22:38,280
hatch. We said we're in very serious trouble.

267
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:42,960
Her bow was noticeably heavier as the ocean waves crashed onto the deck.

268
00:22:43,820 --> 00:22:49,440
Less than two hours later, the Marine Electric was listing to starboard and going down in

269
00:22:49,441 --> 00:22:50,640
29-degree water.

270
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:56,478
So we started to lower the lifeboat, starboard lifeboat.

271
00:22:56,479 --> 00:22:58,780
And just as we were doing that,

272
00:22:58,781 --> 00:23:01,640
the ship capsized right down on her side.

273
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:03,240
In less than a minute, this happened.

274
00:23:03,241 --> 00:23:07,360
The ship made a noise that one of the officers described

275
00:23:07,361 --> 00:23:11,560
like water going down a drain magnified a billion times.

276
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:14,880
It capsized onto its right side,

277
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:17,220
trapping many of the men under the ship,

278
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:19,360
spilling all of them into the ocean.

279
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:24,260
The entire crew of 34 men plunged into the frigid waters

280
00:23:24,261 --> 00:23:26,400
with no survival suits.

281
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:30,760
31 men would die that night.

282
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:34,780
What fatal engineering flaws caused this disaster?

283
00:23:37,760 --> 00:23:43,100
The Marine Electric was a World War II tanker ship, also known as a T-2 tanker.

284
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:45,300
She was nearly 40 years old.

285
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:49,320
They weren't really designed to last forever.

286
00:23:50,380 --> 00:23:54,400
The feeling was you made about 30 trips, it was going to get torpedoed.

287
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:56,100
Who knew what would happen?

288
00:23:56,101 --> 00:24:05,180
After World War II, these ships were used as commercial vessels and oftentimes converted to different uses.

289
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:17,900
In the 1970s and the 1960s, it was common for these vessels to be, in effect, cut in half and enlarged by inserting a midsection.

290
00:24:19,020 --> 00:24:22,420
The common term for this process was jumbo-ize.

291
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:28,180
The Marine Electric was jumboized to 605 feet, nearly twice her original length.

292
00:24:29,300 --> 00:24:35,960
Now, of course, you can probably do this safely if you go back and examine the engineering drawings

293
00:24:35,961 --> 00:24:40,780
and figure out how to properly increase the size and where to supplement the framing of the ship.

294
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,720
I think it's fair to say this was never done.

295
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:50,380
Yet, while jumboizing probably weakened the Marine Electric, it wasn't the flaw that sealed her fate.

296
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:55,340
Chief mate Bob Cusick was part of the Marine Electric crew for five years,

297
00:24:55,700 --> 00:24:57,480
and a mariner for over 40 years.

298
00:24:59,920 --> 00:25:04,680
Just weeks before the disaster, he pointed out innumerable weak spots throughout the ship,

299
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:07,680
including the 40-foot-wide cargo hatch covers.

300
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:14,180
I'd draw up sketches and exactly where it was, the holes,

301
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:16,660
and submit them to the captain.

302
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:19,080
He submitted them to the steamship company.

303
00:25:19,081 --> 00:25:22,280
And one of those things is like a Greek tragedy.

304
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:25,980
The compromised hatch covers were not properly repaired.

305
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:28,860
We'd just keep patching them.

306
00:25:28,861 --> 00:25:34,340
We'd put some duct tape over them and then some, we called it red hand,

307
00:25:34,440 --> 00:25:36,980
where it's like Bondo using a hole in a car.

308
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:41,360
There was over 97 different holes.

309
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:45,280
Those cargo hatch covers would doom the Marine Electric.

310
00:25:45,281 --> 00:25:49,840
But it would take a legal battle to reveal the truth.

311
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:58,140
Bob Cusick, Paul Dewey and Eugene Kelly were the only survivors of a marine electric tragedy and led the crusade.

312
00:25:59,480 --> 00:26:06,920
A steamship company has to show that they didn't send the ship out in unseaworthy condition.

313
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:11,780
It's a big difference if they know about it or they don't know about it.

314
00:26:11,781 --> 00:26:18,780
The bitter battle started with the victims' families on one side and Marine Transport Lines, or MTL,

315
00:26:18,781 --> 00:26:22,780
the owners of the Marine Electric and the Coast Guard on the other.

316
00:26:22,781 --> 00:26:27,780
The official Marine Board of Investigation started the week after the ship sank.

317
00:26:27,781 --> 00:26:36,780
But unlike other investigations, this one was an investigation that had actual witnesses who had survived.

318
00:26:36,781 --> 00:26:46,720
And in this case, all three of them abandoned the code of silence and wanted to tell the true story of how their comrades died and why.

319
00:26:48,320 --> 00:26:53,200
MDL set out to prove that the crew, and especially Bob Cusick, was at fault for the sinking.

320
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:59,300
It hired the best underwater divers with the newest high-tech equipment to examine the wreckage.

321
00:27:00,260 --> 00:27:06,000
They figured if they could show that the crew, namely me, where I was the chief officer,

322
00:27:06,280 --> 00:27:12,940
that I had loaded the ship wrong or hadn't secured the hatches or the anchors or something,

323
00:27:13,380 --> 00:27:14,560
they would have been off the hook.

324
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,860
But it soon became clear that the crew wasn't at fault.

325
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:24,300
The survivors of the Marine Electric revealed that inspections of the ship,

326
00:27:24,301 --> 00:27:27,780
conducted by both the Coast Guard and the American Bureau of Shipping,

327
00:27:27,781 --> 00:27:33,020
were cursory at best, and in some instances, completely false.

328
00:27:34,660 --> 00:27:37,920
The investigation now focused on the weakened hatch covers.

329
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:49,320
Later testimony would show that the Coast Guard certified that the hatch covers were of a good quality and of a good strength

330
00:27:49,321 --> 00:27:52,420
when the hatch covers at that time weren't even on the ship.

331
00:27:52,421 --> 00:27:58,640
What Cusick was able to show on the stand was that the hatch covers were riddled with holes,

332
00:27:58,860 --> 00:28:03,020
that the men were so afraid of the hatch covers that they wouldn't walk on them

333
00:28:03,021 --> 00:28:05,800
for fear of falling to their deaths into the bottom of the ship.

334
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:08,960
They had patched them and patched the patches.

335
00:28:10,040 --> 00:28:13,860
The pounding waves had burst through the disintegrating metal hatch covers

336
00:28:13,861 --> 00:28:16,920
and into the number one and number two cargo holds.

337
00:28:18,500 --> 00:28:21,640
Dominic Colicchio, captain in the U.S. Coast Guard.

338
00:28:22,420 --> 00:28:24,460
former merchant marine,

339
00:28:25,540 --> 00:28:29,680
and part of the Marine Investigation Board analyzing the marine electric sinking,

340
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:31,340
didn't turn a blind eye.

341
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:35,140
He asked the hard-hitting questions and didn't relent under pressure.

342
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:41,820
Because of his tenacity, the final report made an impact well beyond the marine electric case.

343
00:28:42,140 --> 00:28:50,420
Essentially, the report findings said that a great number of the ships in the American fleet were unsound.

344
00:28:51,080 --> 00:28:55,460
The inspections that the Coast Guard had done were inadequate.

345
00:28:55,461 --> 00:29:04,140
And the board also said that the American Bureau of Shipping had a conflict of interest in inspecting these ships

346
00:29:04,141 --> 00:29:07,660
because their fee was in fact paid by the ship owners.

347
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:16,740
Because of Colicchio's determination, mandates were created to improve the shipping industry and prevent similar loss of life.

348
00:29:16,741 --> 00:29:21,860
Well, the legacy of the Marine Electric is a very positive one.

349
00:29:21,861 --> 00:29:27,880
Its loss, arguably the worst loss in American commercial maritime history, produced a lot

350
00:29:27,881 --> 00:29:30,060
of very positive changes.

351
00:29:30,061 --> 00:29:37,100
Number one, there were much tougher Coast Guard inspection standards placed so that

352
00:29:37,101 --> 00:29:40,180
these old ships were removed from the water.

353
00:29:40,181 --> 00:29:46,720
Secondly, ships that plied the North Atlantic during the winter had to carry survival suits

354
00:29:46,721 --> 00:29:52,005
And the third major reform was the institution of the now famous U.S.

355
00:29:52,006 --> 00:29:54,380
Coast Guard rescue swimmer team

356
00:29:54,381 --> 00:29:58,940
that helps people who are in very cold water who can't help themselves.

357
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:07,400
In 2001, a memorial was erected at the Massachusetts Maritime Academy

358
00:30:07,401 --> 00:30:11,280
to the 31 souls who succumbed to the sea that fateful night.

359
00:30:11,281 --> 00:30:19,300
By giving their lives to the ocean, the crew of the Marine Electric actually prevented countless others from suffering the same fate.

360
00:30:20,180 --> 00:30:23,860
They left behind a legacy of reform and justice.

361
00:30:25,500 --> 00:30:30,620
The Marine Electric was not the only T-2 tanker to meet a disastrous fate in frigid waters.

362
00:30:30,621 --> 00:30:42,826
On February 18, 1952, two T-2 tankers broke in half and sank off Cape Cod when their high-sulfur World War II-era steel became brittle and failed during a severe

363
00:30:42,827 --> 00:30:43,660
nor'easter.

364
00:30:44,840 --> 00:30:48,020
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

365
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:58,740
The aviation industry as we know it today has been shaped by ingenuity, daring, and disaster.

366
00:30:59,620 --> 00:31:04,680
One of the most influential disasters occurred the morning of March 31, 1931.

367
00:31:06,440 --> 00:31:14,780
A Fokker F-10A, a tri-motor airplane, was on the field in Kansas City and ready to fly with eight passengers and crew.

368
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:16,820
It would be their final journey.

369
00:31:18,340 --> 00:31:25,800
In fog, ice, and very low cloud cover, Flight 599 suddenly took a nosedive and crashed near Bazar, Kansas.

370
00:31:25,801 --> 00:31:36,560
As we came over that hill right south of us here, we could see the tail section sticking up right out of the middle of a big pile of rubble.

371
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:45,860
All aboard the plane were killed, including one of America's most beloved heroes, University of Notre Dame football coach Newt Rockne.

372
00:31:46,700 --> 00:31:51,920
To this day, he's regarded perhaps as the greatest college football coach that ever lived.

373
00:31:52,520 --> 00:31:55,700
But to this day, he certainly was the greatest of that breed.

374
00:31:55,800 --> 00:32:01,100
Rockne led the Catholic University of Notre Dame to three national championships

375
00:32:01,101 --> 00:32:04,180
and five undefeated seasons during his coaching tenure.

376
00:32:06,100 --> 00:32:07,920
They do not justify defeat.

377
00:32:08,200 --> 00:32:10,660
Those lads do not feel sorry for themselves,

378
00:32:10,900 --> 00:32:14,720
but they stick in there and give the best out of themselves until that last whistle blows.

379
00:32:15,860 --> 00:32:20,280
Rockne boarded the TWA Fokker F-10A at the peak of his popularity.

380
00:32:20,281 --> 00:32:27,280
The United States plunged into mourning when it learned that the sports legend had died.

381
00:32:30,280 --> 00:32:34,280
It didn't take long for the aeronautics branch of the government to begin an investigation.

382
00:32:35,280 --> 00:32:42,280
It wasn't investigated in the most rigorous manner because, let's face it, there wasn't a lot of experience investigating commercial aircrafts.

383
00:32:43,280 --> 00:32:47,280
So there's been a huge amount of speculation about what happened in that crash.

384
00:32:47,281 --> 00:32:54,280
When the Federal Civilian Aircraft Agency attempted to investigate this crash, they continually bungled it.

385
00:32:54,281 --> 00:33:00,280
At that point, when commercial aviation was getting more prevalent and passengers were flying,

386
00:33:00,281 --> 00:33:04,280
and particularly because of the celebrity status of Newt Rockne,

387
00:33:04,281 --> 00:33:10,280
they started looking into why it crashed so that we can prevent this from happening again.

388
00:33:10,281 --> 00:33:14,280
It had been only 28 years since the Wright brothers flew with Kitty Hawk.

389
00:33:14,281 --> 00:33:17,448
Hawk. The predominant use of aircraft was for U.S.

390
00:33:17,449 --> 00:33:20,680
mail delivery, and these planes frequently crashed.

391
00:33:24,700 --> 00:33:29,720
By the late 1920s, new airplanes had been developed that had more powerful motors

392
00:33:29,721 --> 00:33:31,851
and could accommodate passengers.

393
00:33:31,852 --> 00:33:35,920
The airlines were just beginning to coalesce in the late 1920s.

394
00:33:35,980 --> 00:33:37,171
It was very novel.

395
00:33:37,172 --> 00:33:42,000
Remember, it was only in 1927 that Lindbergh flew to Paris from New York,

396
00:33:42,001 --> 00:33:46,100
but the technology was improving literally every month.

397
00:33:46,740 --> 00:33:49,400
The passenger airline industry had begun to emerge,

398
00:33:49,780 --> 00:33:53,960
and one of the most widely used planes was the Fokker F-10A tri-motor.

399
00:33:55,340 --> 00:34:00,540
It was made with a steel body, but it had a wooden wing close to 80 feet long,

400
00:34:01,040 --> 00:34:03,380
and it was wrapped in a light-painted cotton material.

401
00:34:04,260 --> 00:34:07,880
With three Pratt & Whitney 420-horsepower engines,

402
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:13,040
The F-10A flew at a top speed of 135 miles per hour.

403
00:34:14,900 --> 00:34:17,760
Because it had these three engines and all this horsepower,

404
00:34:17,980 --> 00:34:22,260
and because it had this huge wooden wing, it had a lot of lift.

405
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:26,500
The wing was bolted to the fuselage,

406
00:34:26,820 --> 00:34:29,820
and the motors were bolted to maple blocks built into the wing.

407
00:34:31,540 --> 00:34:35,880
Anthony Fokker designed a wooden wing knowing it would be easier and faster to repair.

408
00:34:35,881 --> 00:34:39,820
A metal wing needed special parts and tools.

409
00:34:40,600 --> 00:34:41,640
Not so with wood.

410
00:34:42,740 --> 00:34:44,780
This plane's going to be used all over the world.

411
00:34:44,940 --> 00:34:48,240
If it crashes, you've got to be able to fix it.

412
00:34:48,740 --> 00:34:51,700
So you're going to have a lot more people that are used to dealing with wood.

413
00:34:53,220 --> 00:34:57,420
Unfortunately, this practical decision would doom the most successful plane on the market.

414
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,740
Sifting through the wreckage in the middle of the empty Kansas field,

415
00:35:02,360 --> 00:35:06,140
Experts in the investigation proposed a number of different theories for what happened.

416
00:35:07,760 --> 00:35:10,040
The propeller was broken loose by ice.

417
00:35:10,740 --> 00:35:13,180
The pilot flew it into the ground in a spiral.

418
00:35:13,400 --> 00:35:14,300
It oversped.

419
00:35:14,840 --> 00:35:17,260
There were all sorts of excuses being offered,

420
00:35:17,460 --> 00:35:20,460
none of them the result of good investigatory work of the crash.

421
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:25,060
On May 4, 1931, nearly a month after the crash,

422
00:35:25,061 --> 00:35:28,520
the government banned passengers from flying in Fokker F-10s,

423
00:35:28,521 --> 00:35:32,940
all the while claiming there was nothing inherently wrong with the Fokker aircraft.

424
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:37,420
Finally, the investigators identified the probable cause of the crash.

425
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:41,160
Part of the wooden wing had broken off in mid-flight.

426
00:35:42,320 --> 00:35:45,660
It came fluttering down much like a piece of paper

427
00:35:45,661 --> 00:35:49,780
30 seconds to a minute after the plane had hit the ground over here.

428
00:35:51,020 --> 00:35:55,340
The Fokker wing was comprised of wooden joints that were glued together.

429
00:35:55,341 --> 00:36:01,380
If the wood gets wet with rain or there's moisture that accumulates inside the wing,

430
00:36:01,540 --> 00:36:05,260
it tends to separate and the glue joints start coming apart.

431
00:36:05,720 --> 00:36:08,900
And that is one of the things that they feel happened with this airplane.

432
00:36:09,180 --> 00:36:11,580
So it doesn't have to rot to come apart.

433
00:36:11,700 --> 00:36:14,500
It just has to swell in a way that the glue cannot tolerate

434
00:36:14,501 --> 00:36:17,500
and you break up the wing and you still lose the structural integrity.

435
00:36:18,600 --> 00:36:22,260
The decision to make a wooden wing was a practical one for repairs.

436
00:36:22,860 --> 00:36:27,780
But in reality, such thorough maintenance for the growing fleet of planes and routes was impractical.

437
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:36,340
The government and TWA, who owned the vast majority of the Fokker F-10A airplanes, weathered the disaster with little fallout.

438
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:42,500
However, Anthony Fokker and his airplanes in the U.S. were virtually done in.

439
00:36:43,340 --> 00:36:46,980
Soon, the entire fleet of F-10 planes was disassembled and burned.

440
00:36:46,981 --> 00:36:53,740
No one wanted to fly in them, and new regulations for inspections were too costly to consider.

441
00:36:54,860 --> 00:36:59,960
With the loss of Fokker and his fleet, however, the race was on to engineer a new plane.

442
00:37:01,060 --> 00:37:06,960
Well, if Newt Rocky had never crashed, would we still be flying around in wood Fokker TriMotors?

443
00:37:06,961 --> 00:37:12,080
I don't think so. But the market would have evolved very differently.

444
00:37:12,740 --> 00:37:15,940
Disaster tends to spur technological innovation.

445
00:37:16,980 --> 00:37:28,460
In the aftermath of the Rockne crash, Jack Fry, the owner of TWA, contracted Donald Douglas to build a new, bigger and better plane.

446
00:37:29,040 --> 00:37:35,300
This was the dawn of the DC-3, the plane that many believe was one of the best passenger planes ever made.

447
00:37:39,320 --> 00:37:45,020
The Walt Disney Concert Hall opened on October 23, 2003 in Los Angeles.

448
00:37:45,021 --> 00:37:51,020
It was lauded in the world's press as one of the most beautiful, dramatic, and innovative

449
00:37:51,021 --> 00:37:52,020
structures ever built.

450
00:37:52,021 --> 00:37:59,020
The new Disney concert hall in LA is truly a modern architectural masterpiece in the

451
00:37:59,021 --> 00:38:05,020
sense it's a very freeform structure whose skin is stainless steel.

452
00:38:05,021 --> 00:38:11,020
But there was a small, hard dose of reality in this fantasy land.

453
00:38:11,021 --> 00:38:17,340
embedded in the graceful curves of shining metal was a tiny engineering disaster

454
00:38:17,341 --> 00:38:21,900
superstar architect Frank Gehry designed this new home for the Los Angeles

455
00:38:21,901 --> 00:38:26,260
Philharmonic Orchestra the Disney Concert Hall is part of the music center

456
00:38:26,261 --> 00:38:30,340
complex owned by Los Angeles County

457
00:38:30,341 --> 00:38:35,260
Gehry fashioned the wavy stainless steel exterior to look like a ship with its

458
00:38:35,261 --> 00:38:39,660
sails at full mast a symbolic ceremonial barge to music

459
00:38:39,661 --> 00:38:47,560
He wanted the structure to reflect light in different ways, and so it would be a more interesting shape.

460
00:38:48,640 --> 00:38:53,040
Although some criticized the ambitious design, everyone praised the acoustics.

461
00:38:54,380 --> 00:38:58,740
However, the sail-like designs that reflected sound so beautifully on the interior

462
00:38:58,741 --> 00:39:02,960
caused reflections of a different kind on the hall's steel-clad exterior.

463
00:39:04,480 --> 00:39:08,320
Most of the Disney Concert Hall has a skin that is stainless steel.

464
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:14,500
The brushed portions of the building tend to diffuse the beamed sunlight.

465
00:39:14,501 --> 00:39:21,060
The catch is that with the Disney, there are some surfaces that are polished stainless steel.

466
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:25,300
And they reflect very exactly the sunlight.

467
00:39:26,520 --> 00:39:29,860
Two areas on the building featured polished stainless steel.

468
00:39:30,560 --> 00:39:35,220
The roof of the Founders Room and the marquee above the adjoining Red Cat Theater.

469
00:39:36,200 --> 00:39:39,880
Although these reflective areas only made up about 2% of the building's skin,

470
00:39:40,420 --> 00:39:42,800
they caused positively sizzling problems.

471
00:39:44,020 --> 00:39:46,060
The sections of the wall are concave,

472
00:39:46,640 --> 00:39:51,700
so they focus the sun the same way a parabolic mirror would focus the sun.

473
00:39:52,380 --> 00:39:57,240
And so instead of having just the intensity of the sun reflecting back at you,

474
00:39:57,241 --> 00:40:02,140
you might have the intensity of 20 suns reflecting into your window all at once.

475
00:40:02,760 --> 00:40:04,100
And that's severe.

476
00:40:04,980 --> 00:40:08,700
Nearby condominium residents got a blistering dose of this focused sunlight.

477
00:40:10,120 --> 00:40:13,640
Certain residents were unable to use their own living rooms because of the heat,

478
00:40:14,240 --> 00:40:16,300
and their air conditioning bills skyrocketed.

479
00:40:17,900 --> 00:40:22,880
A flat panel like this, the light will come in, and just like a mirror, will go out parallel.

480
00:40:23,300 --> 00:40:24,306
You'll see the image.

481
00:40:24,307 --> 00:40:27,900
If you're standing over here, you'll see the image of the sun, for example.

482
00:40:28,420 --> 00:40:30,780
If we imagine that this were curved,

483
00:40:31,220 --> 00:40:36,280
then what would happen is all of that light gets focused into some point in front of this.

484
00:40:36,760 --> 00:40:39,860
If it's a very tight curve, the point is very close.

485
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:43,340
If it's a very flat, open curve, the point is very far away.

486
00:40:44,420 --> 00:40:47,020
Not only did adjacent structures get overheated,

487
00:40:47,480 --> 00:40:50,340
the physical space around the concert hall was heating up too.

488
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:55,560
You had measurements being made on the sidewalk of 140 degrees.

489
00:40:55,561 --> 00:41:02,980
If the heat wasn't bad enough, certain traffic intersections were precariously close to receiving a blinding glare,

490
00:41:03,360 --> 00:41:06,040
which could have proven deadly for pedestrians crossing the street.

491
00:41:06,600 --> 00:41:08,020
The county responded immediately.

492
00:41:09,340 --> 00:41:13,240
If you're going to cure the solar concentration problem, you have one or two choices.

493
00:41:13,560 --> 00:41:17,380
You can change geometry of the mirror or destroy the mirror.

494
00:41:17,381 --> 00:41:24,380
A computer model simulated the shifting position of the sun at half hour intervals over a 365-day period.

495
00:41:24,381 --> 00:41:33,380
This study determined which stainless steel panels were the worst offenders, based on time of day and season.

496
00:41:33,381 --> 00:41:44,380
L.A.-based Schiller & Associates conducted the study and assisted Gary in exploring different remedies to the problem.

497
00:41:44,381 --> 00:41:48,180
to the problem.

498
00:41:48,181 --> 00:41:50,380
What we did was we tested different solutions.

499
00:41:50,381 --> 00:41:53,540
We just used one of the panels from the building

500
00:41:53,541 --> 00:41:55,620
and put different films on them.

501
00:41:55,621 --> 00:41:57,200
And we tested each of the surfaces

502
00:41:57,201 --> 00:42:01,240
to see what the different specularity would be.

503
00:42:01,241 --> 00:42:06,100
And some of the surfaces were a big aesthetic change,

504
00:42:06,101 --> 00:42:08,820
may have solved the problem, but would have been a wildly

505
00:42:08,821 --> 00:42:09,940
different building.

506
00:42:09,941 --> 00:42:12,960
I don't know whether you can see the difference in the way

507
00:42:12,961 --> 00:42:15,860
that the different surfaces reflect,

508
00:42:15,861 --> 00:42:18,400
you can see that there are certain moments

509
00:42:18,401 --> 00:42:21,700
when the focal point passes right through your eye.

510
00:42:22,960 --> 00:42:26,080
In March 2005, the sales on the Disney Hall

511
00:42:26,081 --> 00:42:27,960
lost a little of their luster

512
00:42:27,961 --> 00:42:30,940
when work crews began sanding the problematic panels.

513
00:42:33,040 --> 00:42:35,040
The best solution,

514
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:37,740
the one that had the least aesthetic impact,

515
00:42:37,741 --> 00:42:42,080
but still was sufficient, was to sand the surface.

516
00:42:42,081 --> 00:42:50,720
We found that a combination of straight vibrational sanding followed by orbital sanding took care of the specularity.

517
00:42:51,520 --> 00:42:55,620
It took six weeks and $60,000 to complete the process.

518
00:42:56,360 --> 00:42:57,220
Mission accomplished.

519
00:42:58,360 --> 00:42:59,860
Heat and glare dissipated.

520
00:43:01,160 --> 00:43:05,680
Most people cannot tell that it's been treated.

521
00:43:06,640 --> 00:43:09,760
Those who do, I think, seem to find it interesting.

522
00:43:10,440 --> 00:43:13,140
This engineering disaster may have been minor,

523
00:43:13,720 --> 00:43:18,520
but with modern building materials and a growing desire to push the architectural envelope,

524
00:43:19,600 --> 00:43:22,720
new dangers could be right around the next curving corner.

525
00:43:25,200 --> 00:43:28,900
The heat island effect is something that goes on in cities now.

526
00:43:29,280 --> 00:43:34,000
In fact, cities now are so crowded full of inanimate surfaces

527
00:43:34,001 --> 00:43:37,000
that when the sun shines, they get hot.

528
00:43:37,001 --> 00:43:41,320
Now, this actually means that the air in the city starts to rise,

529
00:43:41,620 --> 00:43:47,080
and you actually get sort of air pulled in from around the city,

530
00:43:47,240 --> 00:43:52,540
which helps a tiny bit, but it's also an indicator that the city has overheated itself.

531
00:43:53,500 --> 00:44:00,360
But for today, the Walt Disney Concert Hall stands gracefully in all of its dulled glory.

